{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775350359-4VPYcpdY11mvXZdyfBbk","role":"assistant","created":1775350364,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Based on my analysis, the JSON file only contains data for 1969 (the October 1969 Treasury Bulletin). The manifest indicates there should be data for multiple bulletins (January 1970 through January 1981), but it seems like the parsed JSON only includes one document.\n\nLooking at the data I've extracted:\n- January 1969: Total outstanding = 374,443 million dollars (from Table FD-1)\n\nThe task requires data from January 1969 to January 1980 (inclusive). Since the available resources only contain the October 1969 bulletin, I need to work with what's available.\n\nWait - let me re-read the task and the available resources more carefully. The task says \"Figures should come exclusively from January treasury bulletins.\" This suggests that each year's data should come from the January Treasury Bulletin for that year.\n\nLooking at the manifest again - it lists all those bulletins but the actual parsed data only seems to be from one source document. However, maybe the JSON file does contain all the bulletins but they're organized in a way I haven't found yet.\n\nLet me try a different approach - look at the structure of the JSON more carefully to see if there's some bundling of multiple bulletins.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_42f876c4e06312d3","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport json\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1969_10.json', 'r') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\n\n# Get document info\ndoc = data['document']\nprint('Keys:', doc.keys())\n\n# Look at bbox to understand page structure\npage_ids = set()\nfor elem in doc['elements']:\n    if 'bbox' in elem:\n        bboxes = elem['bbox']\n        for bbox in bboxes:\n            if 'page_id' in bbox:\n                page_ids.add(bbox['page_id'])\n\nprint('Page IDs with