{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775350384-35TIN1FLRXnoU92eUtIo","role":"assistant","created":1775350395,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is puzzling. The task requires data from January 1969 to January 1980, but I only see data for January 1969 in the available files. Let me re-read the manifest and available files more carefully.\n\nWait - maybe I'm overthinking this. Let me look at the problem again. The manifest shows:\n- Multiple source URLs pointing to January bulletins from different years\n- Each source doc has a \"page\" reference\n\nThe files available are limited - but maybe what I need to do is look at what data IS available, and see if maybe the file contains data from MULTIPLE bulletins somehow.\n\nActually wait - let me look at the JSON more carefully. The JSON has page_id in bboxes. Maybe there's a way to segment by page IDs and find January data for different bulletins.\n\nActually, I think I need to reconsider. The manifest shows ALL the source documents are referencing the same files - the October 1969 bulletin. But that's strange because the URLs in the manifest clearly point to January bulletins for different years.\n\nLet me check if perhaps the data is IN the October 1969 bulletin - maybe they include historical data?\n\nLooking back at Table FD-1 in the October 1969 bulletin (page 32), it shows fiscal year data from 1960-1969 plus monthly data for 1968-1969. So it only has up to 1969 data.\n\nWait - maybe the question is asking me to find data that isn't actually available in these files? Let me check what's really in the resource files vs what the manifest says.\n\nActually, wait - the manifest says the source files include treasury_bulletin_1969_10.txt - which is the October 1969 bulletin. But this one bulletin might contain SUMMARY data that includes multiple years.\n\nBut we need January data for each year from 1969 to 1980. The October 1969 bulletin would have data up to August 1969 (the latest month in that bulletin